---
description: Generate repository commit messages using Conventional Commits.
alwaysApply: true
---

When generating a Git commit message, use the Conventional Commits format:

`<type>(<optional scope>): <imperative summary>`

Use a concise, lowercase imperative summary with no trailing period. Keep every
generated line, including the header and each body line, at 100 characters or
fewer. Select the most specific applicable type: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`,
`refactor`, `test`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`, or `perf`. Include a scope when it
clarifies the affected area (for example, `web`, `ui`, `docker`, or `openspec`).

For a breaking change, add `!` before the colon and include a `BREAKING CHANGE:`
footer. If a body is useful, leave one blank line after the subject and explain
the user-visible motivation and impact concisely, wrapping each body line at
100 characters or fewer.

Base the message only on the staged changes. Return the commit message without
Markdown fences, commentary, or alternatives unless explicitly requested.
